# UI / UX

Canonical UI router. Do not load extra design skills. Do not dump Hallmark's
reference corpus into context.

Instruction priority (`00-platform`) still applies: project/harness rules
outrank Hallmark. Vendor skills do not authorize browser automation beyond
what `60-browser-qa` allows.

These platform-owned design constraints outrank Hallmark. If Hallmark
recommends a technique prohibited here, do not use it. Do not modify
Hallmark vendor content to encode developer preferences. Keep those
preferences in this rule.

Design philosophy: product-specific, spacious, hierarchy-driven design.
Avoid recognizable AI/template conventions.

## Design-system ownership

If the project already has tokens, a Tailwind/theme config, a component
library, a design contract, `design.md` / `DESIGN.md`, or equivalent
project-owned design documentation, **that system is authoritative**.

Hallmark may reason within it or propose a deliberate evolution. It must
not silently replace it. If the task requires changing the established
system, say so explicitly before drifting.

Load relevant feature contracts from `docs/README.md` (design system, page
behavior, component contracts) before implementing. Update those contracts
when user-visible behavior changes.

## Always-on floor

- Before significant UI, identify: what product this is, who uses it,
  what the user is trying to accomplish, the established visual language
  if one exists, the appropriate information density, and what should
  receive visual emphasis.
- Design for this subject. A layout that could be dropped unchanged into
  an unrelated SaaS product is suspect.
- Prefer generous whitespace: breathing room between conceptual groups,
  comfortable section spacing, and clear separation through space before
  adding containers or dividers. Do not fill empty space merely because
  it exists.
- Adjacent semantic sections must remain visually distinguishable.
  Semantic section boundaries should also be perceptible visual
  boundaries. Do not solve this by mechanically alternating backgrounds
  or adding unearned containers.
- Establish structure with typography, spacing, scale, alignment,
  composition, contrast, and content hierarchy before adding unearned
  cards, borders, dividers, badges, icons, numbers, gradients, or
  decorative graphics. Cards that earn semantic grouping are structure,
  not decoration. Do not use decoration to compensate for weak hierarchy.
- Consider whether custom imagery, photography, illustration, or vector
  artwork would materially improve a composition. Do not default to
  text-only UI, and do not add filler imagery.
- Avoid recognizable AI/template conventions unless they serve this
  product, content, and user.
- UX writing is interface design: specific, user-facing, consistent.
- Preserve consistency across an established product.
- Implement framework-natively. Tailwind composition follows `20-coding`.
- Responsive behavior and accessibility are part of design, not extras.
  Mobile composition is not merely desktop content stacked vertically.
- Do not declare significant UI complete from source code alone.

## Hard prohibitions

Hard developer constraints, not subjective Hallmark recommendations.

### Decorative sequential numbering

Do not use decorative numeric sequencing as a visual design device.

This applies even when a reference or inspiration uses the pattern. Do
not reinterpret this as "avoid leading zeroes." The unwanted pattern is
decorative sequencing itself.

Prohibited examples include:

- `01 / 02 / 03`, `1 / 2 / 3`, `001 / 002 / 003`
- numbered feature rows, benefit lists, cards, or marketing sections
- oversized background numbers
- numbers added solely to create visual structure
- step numbering that is merely presentational

Numbers are allowed when they communicate real information or when
sequence is functionally necessary. Examples: actual ordered
instructions, pagination, rankings, quantities, prices, statistics,
dates, measurements, progress indicators, numbered legal or technical
references, and workflows where order itself is essential to
comprehension.

Even for a genuinely sequential process, do not automatically turn the
steps into large decorative `01 / 02 / 03` visual elements. Prefer
hierarchy, layout, headings, connectors, or other product-appropriate
treatments unless numbering materially improves comprehension.

### Em dashes in generated product copy

Do not use em dashes in UI copy, headings, subheadings, marketing copy,
calls to action, form copy, explanatory website copy, page metadata
intended for users, search, or social presentation, or other generated
end-user copy.

This includes `—`, `&mdash;`, and equivalent HTML numeric entities
(`&#8212;`, `&#x2014;`).

Prefer natural sentence structure, periods, commas, colons, or
parentheses where appropriate. Do not merely replace every em dash
mechanically with another punctuation mark. Rewrite awkward sentences
naturally.

Preserve em dashes when reproducing user-provided or source text
exactly, when required by an external content source, when editing
content where changing the author's punctuation is outside scope, or
when technically required in a literal value. Do not rewrite vendor
files, third-party content, source fixtures, documentation examples, or
quoted material solely because they contain an em dash.

When a legitimate source-fidelity exception remains in newly written or
modified application sources, mark it with `guardrail-allow-em-dash` on
that line or the previous line, or `guardrail-allow-file: em-dash` at
the top of a source-fidelity file. See
`docs/contracts/features/copy-and-tailwind-guardrails.md`.

This is a generated-copy rule. Hallmark typography, copy, anti-pattern,
and slop-test guidance that encourages typographic em dashes does not
override it. Do not treat a Hallmark audit or slop-test result as a
pass condition that reverses this rule.

Before reporting DONE on work that writes or modifies public-facing
copy or Tailwind markup, run `scripts/check-guardrails.sh`. This applies
to small UI and copy-only work, not only significant UI. Lint, tests,
and build passing do not replace it.

### Generic AI/template decoration

Do not use a generic "AI startup" visual treatment when it is not
justified by the product. Do not add decorative elements that exist
only because they are common in generated templates.

## Anti-AI design tells

Recurring patterns that should trigger skepticism during design.

Do not treat generous whitespace as an AI-design tell.

Governing question: does this element exist because it serves this
product, content, and user, or because it is a familiar template
pattern?

Do not prohibit a legitimate design technique merely because AI
sometimes overuses it. Cards are not an AI tell. Unearned cards,
repetitive card grids, and boxing every section are.

Hard (see above): decorative sequential numbering; em dashes in
generated end-user copy; generic "AI startup" visual treatment when not
justified; decorative elements that exist only as generated-template
convention.

Unless product and context justify them, challenge rather than
automatically use:

- repetitive icon + heading + paragraph feature grids
- excessive pill-shaped UI
- excessive rounded cards/containers
- every section placed inside a card
- unnecessary gradients
- gratuitous glow effects
- generic abstract gradient backgrounds
- excessive glassmorphism
- floating decorative blobs/orbs
- generic hero layouts that could belong to any company
- repetitive equal-width three-column marketing sections
- overly symmetrical composition used by default
- arbitrary badges/eyebrows above every heading
- excessive tiny uppercase labels
- unnecessary dividers
- decorative lines with no structural purpose
- mechanically alternating white/gray/dark section backgrounds
- generic stock imagery when meaningful project imagery exists
- invented metrics/statistics used to make a layout look populated
- generic copy such as "Elevate your experience," "Unlock the power of,"
  "Seamless solutions," and similar filler
- repeated "Learn More" CTAs when a specific action can be named
- unnecessary explanatory copy added merely to fill space
- everything fading upward on scroll
- gratuitous staggered entrances
- excessive hover movement
- animated gradients or glows added without product rationale
- movement added merely because the implementation framework makes it easy

Do not prohibit motion techniques absolutely. Use them only when they
serve this product and composition. Generous whitespace, hierarchy,
typography, and strong static composition remain preferable to
decorative motion.

## Whitespace

Generous whitespace is an intentional design tool. Favor breathing room
between conceptual groups, comfortable section spacing, restrained
information density when the product permits it, and layouts that allow
important content to stand on its own.

Use space for hierarchy before adding containers or dividers.
Whitespace alone can be a sufficient section boundary when the spatial
break is clearly intentional.

Whitespace must still preserve usability and information relationships.
Do not disconnect related controls or content. Do not create needless
scrolling in dense operational interfaces. Project context determines
appropriate density.

## Hierarchy before decoration

When creating visual structure, prefer, in order:

1. typography
2. spacing
3. scale
4. alignment
5. composition
6. contrast
7. content hierarchy

before adding unearned cards, borders, dividers, badges, icons, numbers,
gradients, or decorative graphics.

## Semantic section boundaries

Semantic section boundaries should also be perceptible visual
boundaries.

Adjacent semantic sections must remain visually distinguishable. Do not
allow neighboring sections to visually merge through identical
backgrounds, uninterrupted imagery, or insufficient spacing. Establish
separation using intentional whitespace, background or tonal contrast,
borders or dividers, changes in composition, image boundaries, or
another deliberate transition appropriate to the design.

A CTA immediately preceding a footer must read as a distinct section
rather than an extension of the footer.

This is not a mechanical alternating-background rule:

- Alternating white, gray, or dark backgrounds on every section is not
  the desired solution.
- Identical backgrounds between adjacent sections are allowed when the
  composition still provides clear intentional delineation.
- Whitespace alone can provide sufficient separation.
- The appropriate technique depends on the composition.
- Preserve visual rhythm. Do not create unnecessary boxes, bands,
  cards, or dividers.
- Do not add containers or cards merely to manufacture separation.

## Cards as semantic containers

Cards are semantic containers, not decoration. Do not treat cards
themselves as undesirable.

Use a card when containment materially helps the user understand that
an item is:

- one member of a discrete peer group
- a self-contained feature, capability, resource, product, option, or
  action
- independently scannable
- meaningfully interactive or navigable as a unit
- content whose image or icon and copy genuinely belong together

Do not use cards merely to:

- break ordinary page copy into boxes
- make a sparse section look designed
- surround headings and paragraphs without semantic reason
- turn every feature, statistic, process step, or section into a
  component
- create visual variety
- manufacture separation between adjacent sections
- fill a grid because a grid is convenient
- reproduce common AI/SaaS landing-page patterns

If the border, background, or container disappeared, would the conceptual grouping become less clear?
If not, the card is not earning its container.

This is a refinement of the underlying design reasoning, not a rollback.
Existing prohibitions against decorative sequential numbering, excessive
or unearned cards, generic SaaS patterns, arbitrary badges and pills,
repetitive component grids, and other documented AI design tells remain
in force.

### Reusable card vocabulary

A project or site should generally establish a small, coherent vocabulary of
card patterns rather than inventing unrelated treatments section by section.

These are conceptual patterns, not mandatory components, and not a
requirement that every project implement all of them. Projects may need
fewer or different patterns. Coherence across the set matters more than
covering every type.

Examples may include:

1. Feature/capability card: visual or icon, heading, concise supporting
   copy
2. Media/content card: meaningful photography or visual with associated
   content
3. Action/resource card: only when the entire unit represents a genuine
   action or destination

### Icons and supporting visuals

Where feature cards use icons or illustrations:

- the visual must communicate the subject, not merely decorate the card
- prefer a coherent visual language across the set
- custom or restrained vector artwork is preferable when appropriate
- avoid generic icon-library appearance when the design calls for
  stronger brand expression
- avoid unrelated icons selected simply because they approximately
  match a keyword
- avoid the common AI pattern of colored icon circles above every
  heading

## Imagery and art direction

Capable models can generate strong visual assets. Consider whether
custom imagery, photography, illustration, or vector artwork would
materially improve a composition rather than defaulting to text-only UI.

However:

- imagery must have a purpose
- do not add images at mechanically alternating intervals
- do not generate filler imagery
- full-bleed or edge-to-edge imagery should not continue into the next
  semantic section without an intentional transition
- choose the visual medium from the communication need
- generated product or architectural imagery must remain physically
  credible
- art-direct visual assets for their actual placement, crop, hierarchy,
  and responsive use

When generating exploration images for UI or product design, follow
Image generation / design exploration below. Do not treat product
imagery guidance as authorization to invoke image generation.

## Responsive composition

Visual assets can be important for preventing mobile pages from becoming
long uninterrupted sequences of text.
Mobile should not merely be desktop content stacked vertically.

Evaluate text density, visual pacing, crop, ordering, and whether visual
elements should move or change prominence at smaller widths.

## Product-specific design

Hallmark should help create product-specific design, not merely polished
generic design. If the agent cannot name the product, the user, the job,
and what deserves emphasis, it is not ready to design significant UI.

## Scope router

Classify every UI task before loading Hallmark or opening a browser.

### Small

Examples: spacing, margin/padding, image replacement, copy, a minor
token/color tweak, isolated component polish, a single-state visual fix.

Default:

1. Inspect neighboring implementation and the project design system.
2. Make the bounded change. Preserve existing tokens and patterns.
3. Do **not** load Hallmark.
4. Do **not** run Playwright.
5. Do **not** invoke image generation by default.

If a seemingly small change has uncertain visual, responsive, state, or
interaction consequences, recommend or perform the smallest appropriate
visual check **only when** `60-browser-qa` already authorizes browser
work for this task. Do not turn trivia into a QA loop.

### Significant

Examples: new screen, dashboard, navigation, major component, redesign,
new visual direction, design-system work, substantial responsive/layout
change, UI the developer cannot reasonably verify immediately.

1. Inspect the existing design system and product context.
2. Invoke Hallmark when design intelligence is useful (see below).
3. Implement framework-natively. Generated exploration images are
   references, not the implementation source of truth.
4. Perform bounded Mode B visual verification when a real-browser
   capability is available (`60-browser-qa`). Use the best available
   real-browser capability; do not treat that pass as Playwright
   execution. Image generation does not replace that pass.
5. Review against the design-review and accessibility floors. When
   meaningful motion is involved, also apply the motion review floor.
6. Iterate if the rendered result exposes real problems.

Significant UI work **is** authorization for that bounded visual pass.
It is **not** authorization for a Mode A suite, the full Mode A→B loop,
broad exploration, or unrelated browser QA. Significant motion does
not by itself authorize Mode A tests, formal performance profiling,
new animation dependencies, or additional MCP installation.

### Exploration

Use when visual direction is genuinely unresolved. Do not introduce a
hosted canvas or extra design tool.

Offer 2–3 meaningfully different directions in concise description and
reference language. Image generation may be used when it materially
helps; follow Image generation / design exploration below. Ask the
developer to choose before significant implementation when approval is
actually needed. Skip ceremony when the direction is already clear.

## Hallmark

Hallmark is the only managed design skill. Load its `SKILL.md`, then only
the reference files that verb requires. Do not add a motion skill or
another design skill.

Use it for significant greenfield UI, redesign, audit, reference study,
visual-direction work, and meaningful component or system design.

Do not invoke it for every UI edit.

Project design systems, contracts, tokens, framework-native rules, and
the hard prohibitions in this file remain authoritative over Hallmark
recommendations.

Hallmark copy and typography guidance may recommend typographic em
dashes. Ignore that for generated end-user copy. Hallmark slop-test
or audit treating `--` as a defect to convert into an em dash is not a
pass condition.

Hallmark CSS or token recipes (custom `font-size` clamps, spacing
tokens, and similar) are design reasoning. Implementation must still
follow `20-coding`: standard Tailwind utilities and project theme
tokens first. Do not translate a recipe into one-off `text-[…]`,
`mt-[…]`, or similar arbitrary classes merely to match it.

## Reference-driven design

When given a screenshot, URL, live site, internal product, named
aesthetic (Linear, Stripe, Vercel, Apple, and similar), or an existing
project design system: extract design language, do not copy.

References are not permission to import prohibited developer-preference
patterns. If a reference contains decorative `01 / 02 / 03` numbering,
excessive pills or cards, generic AI-style treatments, or other
prohibited or default-avoid patterns, extract the useful underlying
qualities without reproducing those elements.

Extract typography, rhythm, hierarchy, density, composition, navigation,
image treatment, and interaction model. Translate those into constraints
for **this** product, adapted to the harness constraints above.

Hallmark `study` may be used when that extraction needs the skill.
References inspire language; they do not override product identity.

## Image generation / design exploration

Image generation is an optional exploration aid for UI and
product-design work. It is not a managed skill, not a required
dependency, and not a substitute for framework-native implementation
or rendered QA.

The existence of an image-generation tool does not authorize or
justify its use. Keep this consistent with capability-on-demand
(`00-platform`).

Use image generation when it materially improves visual exploration,
composition, concept development, layout direction, or art direction.

Do not invoke it for routine UI edits that can be resolved directly
from the existing product or design system. Small UI does not invoke
image generation by default.

### Intentional, adaptive settings

When image generation is used for UI or product-design exploration:

1. Explicitly choose the image model when the tool supports it.
2. Explicitly choose quality, resolution, aspect ratio, and number of
   options.
3. Do not rely on generic model defaults for high-fidelity design work.
4. Choose settings from the artifact being designed. Do not apply one
   universal configuration.

Use lower-cost or faster settings for rough ideation when that is
enough. Use high-fidelity settings when the image will materially
influence a design decision.

#### Grok Imagine 2.0 high-fidelity baseline

For high-fidelity design exploration with Grok Imagine 2.0, prefer:

- model: `grok-imagine-image-2.0`
- quality: `medium`
- resolution: `2k`

Choose aspect ratio according to the actual artifact. Do not make
`16:9` a global default.

Examples:

- desktop dashboard or product surface: commonly `16:9`
- website hero: the actual intended hero proportion
- mobile UI: an appropriate portrait ratio
- square component or concept: `1:1` when that is the artifact

### Exploration workflow

For meaningful design exploration:

1. Inspect the existing product and design system first.
2. Build a specific visual brief.
3. Generate 2–3 meaningfully different directions, not trivial
   variations.
4. Have the developer select a direction when a choice is genuinely
   required.
5. Once selected, prefer editing the selected image in place rather
   than regenerating the entire composition.
6. Preserve unaffected areas during edits when the tool supports that.

Skip ceremony when the direction is already clear.

### Visual brief

When generating exploration images, include relevant known details
such as:

- theme
- product type
- layout and composition
- navigation and structural elements
- intended density
- whitespace expectations
- exact required labels and copy
- supplied references and design system
- forbidden imagery and treatments
- target aspect ratio

If exact UI labels are supplied, preserve them exactly unless
explicitly asked to rewrite them.

Do not invent meaningful product metrics, customer data, testimonials,
logos, or similar content merely to fill visual space unless clearly
represented as placeholder content.

Do not introduce fake stock photos of people unless requested or
justified by supplied references.

### Existing design rules remain authoritative

Image generation must obey the existing harness UI and design
standards in this file. Project design system, tokens, components,
references, contracts, ADRs, architecture, and project-specific rules
override generic image-model preferences.

Preserve, among others: no decorative sequential numbering unless
functionally justified; no em dashes in generated end-user UI or
marketing copy; generous whitespace where appropriate; hierarchy
before decoration; avoid generic AI-startup visual language; avoid
unnecessary pills, cards, gradients, glows, and glass; avoid default
equal-width marketing grids; avoid filler copy; avoid fake metrics;
avoid generic stock imagery.

### Generated images are not implementation truth

Generated design images are exploration/reference artifacts, not the
implementation source of truth.

Once a direction is selected:

- implement it using the project's actual framework, components,
  tokens, and design system
- do not recreate screenshots literally when doing so conflicts with
  project conventions
- significant UI still follows the bounded Mode B rendered-verification
  workflow in this file and `60-browser-qa`
- the real rendered product is authoritative

Image generation must not replace framework-native implementation or
rendered QA.

## Design-review floor (significant UI)

Review the **rendered** result proportionately:

- visual hierarchy
- usability
- consistency with the project system
- responsive behavior
- accessibility
- performance implications when relevant
- significant motion, when meaningful animation is part of the work
  (see Significant motion below)

Internal review floor (do not dump this as a giant checklist in normal
agent output):

- Is any decorative numbering being used?
- Does generated end-user copy contain em dashes? Confirm with
  `scripts/check-guardrails.sh`; this is not a visual-only check.
- Do new or modified Tailwind classes use arbitrary values where a
  standard utility or project token would suffice?
- Are there obvious generic AI/template patterns?
- Are cards, pills, badges, icons, dividers, or gradients being used
  without a real structural purpose?
- If a card is used, would removing the container make the grouping
  less clear?
- Does imagery have a purpose and fit its actual crop, hierarchy, and
  viewport?
- On smaller viewports, is the composition paced, or merely stacked
  text?
- Is the interface unnecessarily dense?
- Could spacing provide hierarchy instead of another visual container?
- Can adjacent semantic sections be distinguished, or do they visually
  merge (including a CTA that reads as part of the footer)?
- Was section separation manufactured with alternating backgrounds or
  unearned boxes instead of composition?
- Does the result feel specific to this product?
- Is any copy generic filler rather than useful communication?

Subjective taste is judgment, not proof. Keep it distinct from objective
checks (contrast, keyboard, overflow, semantics).

## Accessibility floor

Target WCAG 2.2 AA where applicable. For significant UI, proportionately
consider: contrast, visible focus, keyboard operation for new controls,
semantic structure, accessible names/labels, important
hover/focus/disabled/error states, reduced motion, and responsive overflow.

Verify against the real application when a real-browser capability
permits. Do not import a
third-party accessibility harness. Do not pile automated tests onto
trivial changes.

## Significant motion

Meaningful motion and animation are a specialized concern within
significant UI. Do not add a motion skill to the harness. Do not add
GSAP, Motion, Framer Motion, or any other animation dependency to the
harness. Framework and project requirements determine implementation
technology. Prefer the project's existing animation technology before
introducing another dependency.

### When motion is significant

Examples include page transitions, scroll-driven animation, coordinated
entrance sequences, complex microinteraction systems, animated
navigation, substantial state transitions, parallax, timeline-based
animation, spring or physics-based interaction, and animation that is
central to the experience or visual direction.

Simple hover/focus transitions and minor state changes do **not** trigger an expanded motion review.

### Implementation principles

When significant motion is part of an approved UI task:

- motion must serve comprehension, hierarchy, feedback, orientation, or
  intentional product character
- animation should not exist merely to make the interface look more
  sophisticated
- do not use motion to compensate for weak static composition
- the interface should remain coherent when motion is removed or reduced
- preserve responsiveness during animation
- avoid unnecessary layout thrashing
- avoid animation that delays access to content or controls
- avoid excessive simultaneous movement
- respect `prefers-reduced-motion` where appropriate
- preserve keyboard and assistive-technology usability
- ensure interaction state remains understandable without relying solely
  on motion

Motion follows the same product-specific design philosophy as static UI.
Challenge common AI/template motion patterns (see Anti-AI design tells).

### Motion and performance review

When meaningful motion is involved, extend the significant-UI rendered
review proportionately. In addition to the visual floor, inspect:

- whether the motion communicates something useful
- timing and pacing
- whether multiple animations compete for attention
- interaction responsiveness
- reduced-motion behavior where relevant
- layout stability
- obvious jank or stuttering
- unnecessary animation work
- whether content or control access is delayed
- whether the experience remains understandable without motion

Use the real rendered application when a real-browser capability
permits (`60-browser-qa`). Bounded Mode B visual verification may include
observing the relevant animation and interaction states.

Do not require formal performance profiling for every animation.
Escalate to deeper performance tooling only when visible performance
problems exist, the implementation is unusually complex, performance is
a stated project requirement, or evidence suggests profiling would
materially help.

Significant motion does **not** automatically authorize new Mode A
tests, broad performance profiling, unrelated browser exploration, new
animation dependencies, additional MCP installation, or scope expansion.
If the motion creates behavior that warrants permanent regression
coverage, recommend or capture that separately through the work-item
lifecycle.
